)

# Ed25519's verification rules have no published vectors, because they are
# not one rule — RFC 8032 §7.1 gates the scheme and cannot gate the choice,
# every case in it being an honestly produced signature that every rule
# accepts. C2SP's set is built the other way round: each case is an edge
# case in `A` or `R` chosen to make implementations disagree, carrying the
# flags that say which. That is what turns a rule into something testable
# (`sig_frx/classical/testing/ed25519_cctv_vectors.py`).
http_file(
    name = "cctv_ed25519_vectors",
    downloaded_file_path = "ed25519vectors.json",
    sha256 = "b38e84caf3e7e89170ff520292dbeae421b0a794c27408ce5ce973018fe3d7f9",
    urls = ["https://raw.githubusercontent.com/C2SP/CCTV/1e3d2860d46e94e777e1b17c7a6f2436387e3ecc/ed25519/ed25519vectors.json"],
)

# BIP-327 publishes one vector file per protocol stage rather than one file
# per operation, which is what a multi-round protocol needs: a wrong binding
# factor and a wrong partial signature are different bugs, and gating only the
# final signature would report both as the same failure. Each file carries its
# own `error_test_cases` beside `valid_test_cases`, so the negative half of the
# gate arrives with the positive half instead of being invented here. Each file
# is declared by the change that first reads it, all from upstream commit
# ccb5415095c5b096abc9bd72384a6db4b7c34bce: a pin nothing depends on is a
# sha256 the build never checks against a byte of real data.
